Fulgorhythm
Ingredients: AFROBEAT + HYPNOTIC, TRANCE-LIKE & MINIMAL + Melodica + 1950s ERA
Creative Catalyst: The genre honors an offshore pirate radio station on the Gulf of Guinea that only achieved broadcast voltage when struck directly by lightning during equatorial thunderstorms.

Fulgorhythm is a storm-locked collision where Fela Kuti's polyrhythmic engine is starved of brass excess and boiled down into an austere, repetitive trance pulse. Through the heavy hiss of 1950s magnetic wire-recorders and warm tube-limiter saturation, a single plastic melodica wheezes like a drowning accordion, playing cyclical, modal incantations over bone-dry talking drums and subterranean thunder-rumble.

Genre DNA
* Tempo & Pulse: 104–112 BPM. A hypnotic, circular 12/8 cross-rhythm driven by stripped-down Tony Allen-style rimshots and muted shekere beads, rigidly locked into an unchanging groove that refuses drum fills or brass fanfare.
* Harmonic Palette: Somber Phrygian and Dorian modal loops. Repetitive two-chord vamps executed on flatwound-strung archtop guitars, anchored by a deep sub-acoustic upright bass drone and suspended 1950s West African palm-wine intervals.
* Production Signatures: (1) Spark-gap plate reverb that blossoms and decays unpredictably, simulating atmospheric ionization; (2) Extreme ribbon-mic saturation mimicking overdriven 1950s vacuum-tube transmitters; (3) Barometric side-chain compression, where the entire mix ducks whenever low-frequency lightning rumbles trigger the master bus.
* Dynamics: Monolithic accretion and sudden evaporation. Tracks assemble microscopic ostinatos over ten to fifteen minutes, holding listeners in an unbroken, sweat-drenched stasis until a sudden surge in tape hiss resets the rhythmic phase.

Scene & Origin Lore
In 1954 along the storm-battered coast of Victoria Island, Lagos, an illegal amateur broadcaster known only as Radio Keraunos hoisted a zinc-braided copper antenna atop an abandoned oil palm barge. The transmitter had no generator; it drew its lethal anode voltage solely from direct lightning strikes during torrential squalls, hurling unearthly, hyper-compressed highlife jams across the Bight of Benin for twenty minutes at a time before going dead. In the shoreline shanties and dockside warehouses, a secret youth cult called the Ọlọ́run-Wire gathered during storm warnings, dressed in damp, starch-stiffened 1950s gabardine suits, bakelite cufflinks, and rubber-soled deck shoes. Armed with modified green plastic melodicas and battery-powered tube amplifiers, they held silent vigils until the lightning hit the water, instantly launching into a feverish, circular trance-dance known as gbá-tútù (the cold-sweep) that synchronized their footwork with the station's intermittent, broadcast-frequency pulse.

Typical Instruments: Green plastic Hohner melodicas (fitted with copper ground-wires), Yoruba dundun (talking drum), iron agogô bells, shekere, 1950s Gibson L-5 archtop guitars through overdriven Fender Tweed Deluxe tube amps, RCA 44-BX ribbon microphones, and custom spark-gap transmitter noise generators.

For Fans Of: Augustus Pablo, Tony Allen (at his most minimalist), William Onyeabor, Steve Reich (Drumming era).

