Track 3: Gbá-Tútù on the Flooded Pier
- Story & Context: Thirty years after Radio Keraunos went down in a catastrophic gale, an elderly Commodore Adebayo gathered the surviving cultists at the sunken wooden pier of Bar Beach during the heaviest monsoon of 1984. As the midnight cloudburst filled their ear-horns with cold brine and the smell of charred engine oil, they waited without instruments for the dead frequency to speak through the salt-crusted iron ruins. When lightning struck the exposed rebar beneath the waves, an impossible 108 BPM sub-bass resonance rattled the marrow of their collarbones, followed by the faint, reedy cry of a long-drowned melodica echoing off the water. The elders locked wet arms and circled the pier in the pouring rain, murmuring their sacred hook "the sky forgets nobody who stayed in the rain" until the sun broke through the storm clouds at dawn.
